=== "When is a pearl not a pearl?"

I carefully checked that my ring of slow digestion
"wasn't metal" before keeping it on while I went
swimming in some pools I'd made to turn useless
potions into fodder for a water prayer.

     [Cursed water doesn't seem to dilute into
     uncursed water. I thought I remembered
     otherwise. Oh, well, the prayer made it
     blessed.]

When next I checked my inventory, I was wearing a
"thoroughly rusty ring of slow digestion".

Huh? Pearls have their _existence_ in the water,
they shouldn't "rust".

I'm guessing that the "pearl" was a mounted stone on
some rust-susceptible metal ring, rather than the
whole ring being carved from a single pearl?

How should I have expected this?

No harm done, of which I know, having that ring be
rusty, it's just, I'm curious.

=== Some swearing and cursing.

Grrr. Cheap-jack dungeon jewelers anyway, itty-bitty
barely visible pearl, base metal ring, ... good
thing for their life expectancy the ring is magic
and useful.

== Rnd00172 the Student of Stones

N.B., about my ongoing monk game, which I'm only
playing when I'm mildly coherent, so I haven't
gotten very far since I last mentioned that game,
nor managed to kill him off quite yet.

I just found out (by using an uncursed marker and
him scribing a lot of Identify scrolls on blessed
blank scrolls until I finally got a full inventory
identification to happen) that I'd scored him a
spell book of Identify. Yay!

Overall, probably at least 1/3rd in each case of all
possible scrolls, rings, spell books and potions are
now known.

I'm a bit bummed by how little help blessed lenses
are for reading spell books. I was expecting better,
so I could save on dipping spell books into Holy
Water.

This Identify spell will make life easier, once his
casting skills bring that spell within the realm of
the possible for my XL:11 monk.

Does further enchanting his robe make spell casting
easier for him, or is the spell casting boost from
wearing a robe some fixed amount, with enchanting
the robe only affecting AC?

He's a pitifully bad spell-caster without it, and
being a monk, his MAX PW right now is only 51, so
he runs out of gas for spell casting quickly, too.

Still, I need to pay better attention and use up
manna as fast as it accumulates, since he's not
using it for combat, rather than letting him sit
with manna at max and so accumulating none.

Wearing the robe, he can cast polymorph at an 88%
failure rate.

Hmmmm. What would I like to polymorph?

I wonder if that polymorph spell works on pets?

Does it zap a beam, like the wand does?

I'd like to get something with a better life
expectancy than his current war horse, they tend to
starve so easily (this one even turned up his nose
at a slime mold; I thought they _liked_ molds), and
if possible upgrade the large cat and large dog,
too.

My monk can cast remove curse at 0% failure with his
robe on, so we're having fun wielding and uncursing
stray crud found to be cursed by dropping it on his
stash altar. That's how his ring of slow digestion
mentioned above became uncursed.

With Excaliber and Frost Brand, he's long ago given
up role playing and gone into slash and bash mode,
though popcorn monsters to unarmed combat attacks
get kicked to death instead, in hopes of improving
his skills there.

I'm wasting too much time having him use a long
sword whose skill is already maxed at basic, just
because watching monsters fall fast is fun, and not
enough time accumulating successful hits in unarmed
combat to get him better skill levels, which also
are still at basic.

He has a naked AC of 1, and a dressed-out AC with no
body armor at around -14 (used to be -18, but rust
and rot have intervened as they so frequently do;
whoever was arguing against foo-proofing armor and
weapons is full of hot air).

He's got a big stash of other unread spell books,
soon to become well-read spell books.

His main stash is on DLVL 5, sitting on a co-aligned
altar in a chest. That's probably too high in the
dungeon to be his permanent stash, so if we find an
altar further down, a new stash will start there.

There are up-stairs right next to the altar, and up
those stairs and about ten steps away are the pools
he made for diluting potions and blanking scrolls.

Some helpful dwarf excavated away a big chunk of the
stash level. I prefer a stash/altar level to be
leveled border to border to eliminate "comes around
a corner" surprises, and I'd rather let some
peaceful burrower do all the work for me. Him being
lawful, a _lot_ of the dwarfs get created peaceful.

This also left lots of boulders for making a fort
around the altar if I decide that pudding farming
would be a fun exploit. The only reason to do that
would probably be for smoky potions and magic
markers, he isn't all that far from kitted out in
terms of surviving down to the castle, so doesn't
need a lot of other stuff, but those two sure do
come in handy, and potions to dilute and turn into
holy water are handy too. If magic markers don't
death drop, well, he's gathering horns from
cross-aligned unicorns slowly but surely.

He's got two bags of holding, one from Sokoban, one
found on the floor, and three plain sacks so far, so
he still can't nest stuff in an inner bag of
holding, which looks pretty dangerous to me anyway.

He's got teleportitus, which was acquired
deliberately, as it's more useful than it is a
nuisance, quite often.

     [Though teleportitus does mean that his player
     has actually to bother to watch what's happening
     on the screen with each move, or risk losing the
     PC. Yawn. That unwillingness to pay strict
     attention to the game is definitely a weakness
     of mine which is a big part of the reason no PC
     of mine has ever ascended.]

He's got a couple of smoky potions, which
conveniently happen to be gain ability, so I'm
hoping to bless them in hopes of scoring him some
much needed reflection in the form of silver dragon
scale mail.

Which, of course, will make him clumsy as spit.

Should I go for the reflection amulet, instead?

I'm not quite sure what the survivability of monks
is without body armor as the game gets tough, but
I'm fairly sure he scored a cursed Hawaiian shirt
off a DLVL 7 bones pile that can be used to improve
his AC a bit extra.

He's got a blessed luckstone and a blessed
touchstone, which latter means he's carrying a lot
of precious gem clutter, but the diamonds are handy
for scribing Elbereth in the rock.

He's got a magic harp, which, if I remember, lets
him tame adjacent monsters.

He knows what a scroll of enchant armor is, but
doesn't yet recognize scrolls of enchant weapon,
which I'd really like to have him use on Excaliber
to kick up its lethality.

There's several dangerous scrolls he doesn't yet
recognize, so "read it and see what it does" isn't
yet an option.

Oh, and come Master Kaen, if by chance he gets that
far, there's this conveniently IDed wand of Death
he's carrying, and a scroll of charging he can
uncurse and then bless to make sure it has some
charges in it when the time comes.

Now if only I understood the trick that lets him go
down stairs with him still getting the first move at
the bottom, for that point in the quest.

I'm sure it is a mix of maxed speed (he's fast, and
he has some pretty horridly corroded speed boots
that make him a bit faster) and somehow evening up
the turn versus move counter at the stair top before
descending, but the details of doing that escape me.

